      "id": 1,
      "text": "Timberville’s earliest white settlers were Pennsylvania Dutch who migrated to the Shenandoah Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timberville%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before a town existed, two forts guarded the area: one on Fort Run in 1744 and another by the Shenandoah River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townoftimberville.com/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A log house erected in 1750 on the second fort’s site still stands today, a rare 18th‑century survivor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townoftimberville.com/history/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Plains District Memorial Museum near Timberville lets visitors explore the town’s and the Shenandoah Valley’s rich past.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townoftimberville.com/history/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Main Street’s historic layout, once captured in old photographs, showcases Timberville’s 19th‑century commercial heart.",
